    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4062",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a,$ given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$",
      "worked_solution": "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1,$ the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -1.$\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b =1.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2.\n$$",
